    WHY_PROMPT_CODE["<b>WHY TREAT PROMPTS AS PROGRAMS?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> the prompt branches on OS, mode, capabilities, security, and configured tools. Hiding that logic in a template DSL makes control flow hard to test and review.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> Markdown owns static prose; Python owns conditions, ordering, validation, metrics, and composition.<br/><b>Why not Jinja variants or one giant string:</b> template branches create a second programming language; giant strings make section-level testing and budgeting opaque.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> ordinary debugging and unit tests; prompt changes remain production code and require regression discipline."]:::why
    WHY_ORDER["<b>WHY THIS SYSTEM-PROMPT ORDER?</b><br/><b>Identity/precedence first</b> establishes authority. <b>Mode</b> defines the social contract before affordances. <b>Shell</b> prevents platform-invalid actions. <b>Security before routing/tools</b> frames capability with constraints. <b>Routing before reference</b> teaches choice, not merely availability. <b>Autonomy</b> governs multi-step persistence. <b>Interaction</b> defines legitimate pause. <b>Critical rules last</b> exploit recency for high-cost failure modes.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> the prompt is an ordered policy stack, not a bag of instructions; reordering can change behavior even with identical text."]:::why
    WHY_SECTIONS["<b>WHY INCLUDE BOTH TOOL SCHEMAS AND TOOL GUIDANCE?</b><br/><b>Schema</b> says what arguments are syntactically legal. <b>Routing/reference policy</b> says when a tool is appropriate, which safer abstraction precedes shell, and what evidence to gather next.<br/><b>Why not schemas alone:</b> the model may choose a legal but unsafe/inefficient route. Why not prose-only pseudo-tools: provider-native validation and structured decoding are lost.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> machine-checkable calls plus human-readable decision policy; duplicated descriptions consume tokens and must stay aligned."]:::why
    WHY_EXAMPLES["<b>WHY CAPABILITY-GATED WORKED EXAMPLES?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> examples teach multi-tool choreography more effectively than abstract rules, but they are expensive and can make chat/plan modes imitate edits they cannot perform.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> include edit-heavy examples only for agent mode, enabled editor, and models not classified small.<br/><b>Why not always include them:</b> small contexts lose useful history; restricted modes receive contradictory demonstrations.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> examples are treated as an optimization with a budget, not sacred prompt content."]:::why
    WHY_DYNAMIC["<b>WHY STABLE POLICY FIRST AND DYNAMIC DATA NEAR THE USER TURN?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> repo state, MCP availability, status, and context packet change frequently. Putting them inside the primary system string invalidates provider prefix caches and makes historical dynamic snapshots look like permanent policy.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> fingerprint the stable leading system prefix + tool schemas; rebuild volatile application-owned messages once per request beside the latest user turn.<br/><b>Why not append dynamic state forever:</b> stale maps/status accumulate and compete with current truth.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> cache reuse and clearer authority; message ordering must be normalized for provider compatibility."]:::why
  end

