    WHY_PROVIDER["<b>WHY OWN A PROVIDER-NORMALIZATION BOUNDARY?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> providers differ in streaming chunks, tool-call IDs, stop reasons, errors, parameters, caching, and endpoint conventions.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> native client families translate at one boundary into Grinta messages, usage, tool calls, and exceptions.<br/><b>Why not scatter if-provider branches through the agent:</b> orchestration behavior would change with transport details and every new model would touch core logic. Why not trust one universal wrapper blindly: lowest-common-denominator abstractions often hide meaningful semantics.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> portable engine and explicit compatibility; the adapter/catalog surface requires continuous maintenance."]:::why
    WHY_STRICT["<b>WHY STRICT PROVIDER RESOLUTION?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> model names are not globally unique and compatible gateways may expose arbitrary IDs. Guessing from substrings can send credentials or unsupported parameters to the wrong endpoint.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> explicit provider prefix/config, otherwise exact catalog lookup; ambiguity fails visibly.<br/><b>Why not fuzzy convenience:</b> a friendly misroute is more dangerous than a clear configuration error.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> deterministic security and support behavior; onboarding may require one explicit setting."]:::why
    WHY_CAPABILITY["<b>WHY DATA-DRIVEN CAPABILITIES INSTEAD OF MODEL-NAME CONDITIONS?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> context size, parallel calls, parameter support, and pricing are properties that change independently of provider brand.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> catalog facts drive budgets and request shaping; provider-family code handles protocol semantics.<br/><b>Why not checks like name contains claude:</b> aliases, versions, and compatible hosts make folklore brittle.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> most compatibility updates become auditable data changes; stale catalogs remain an operational risk and conservative fallbacks are required."]:::why
    WHY_STREAM_WAL["<b>WHY A SECOND WRITE-AHEAD CHECKPOINT FOR LLM STREAMING?</b><br/><b>Problem:</b> after a crash mid-stream, the remote provider may have consumed the request and emitted partial content, but the local engine cannot know whether repeating it will duplicate cost or actions.<br/><b>Chosen:</b> write a small intent marker before the call and delete only after complete decode; recent uncommitted markers block guessing.<br/><b>Why not persist the full prompt:</b> secrets, size, and duplication of the event ledger. Why not retry automatically: the outcome is ambiguous, not proven failed.<br/><b>Consequence:</b> honest recovery and crash evidence; some sessions require explicit operator resolution."]:::why
  end

